{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Selectively log network activity events by identity in AWS CloudTrail - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced enhanced event filtering for network activity events related to Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) endpoints in CloudTrail. This new feature allows customers to selectively log network activity events based on the identity of the IAM user making the API call. Users can configure selectors to log only specific events, such as access denied events, when the calling identity is not on a trusted list. This helps in reducing logging costs and filtering out routine traffic from trusted identities. The UserIdentity filtering feature can be used to focus on network activity event logging for security scenarios and detect potential data exfiltration attempts through VPC endpoints. Users can combine UserIdentity conditions with existing fields like eventName or vpcEndpointId for fine-grained control over what gets recorded. The feature is available via the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs in all AWS Regions where CloudTrail network activity events are supported. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail user guide or read the AWS Blog on Network Activity Events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-cloudtrail-filter-useridentity-advance-selectors/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon CloudWatch now supports cross-region telemetry auditing and enablement rules - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudWatch has introduced the capability to audit and enable telemetry configuration for various AWS services like Amazon EC2, Amazon VPC, and AWS CloudTrail across multiple AWS Regions from a single region. This feature allows customers to apply enablement rules to selected regions or all available regions at once. The rules can be scoped to specific regions or all supported regions, ensuring consistent telemetry collection. The cross-region telemetry configuration and enablement rule is available in all AWS commercial regions, and standard CloudWatch pricing applies for telemetry ingestion. Customers can create organization-wide enablement rules for specific services, which automatically expand to include new regions as they become available","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-cloudwatch-cross-region-enablement-rules/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking CloudTrail docs on AdvancedEventSelector field selector logic and limits."}

