{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Streaming data of unknown size to Amazon S3 with multipart upload","context":"### How multipart upload handles a stream of unknown size\n\nMultipart uploads don't require the object's total size. Each part declares its own **Content-Length** only. The writer receives the **Content-Length** for each part from the bytes in its buffer. The optional **x-amz-mp-object-size** header on the [CompleteMultipartUpload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_CompleteMultipartUpload.html) API operation declares the total size.\n\nThe upload uses the following API calls:\n\n1. The [CreateMultipartUpload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_CreateMultipartUpload.html) API operation starts the upload and returns an upload ID, **UploadId**. The request has no size parameter.\n2. The [UploadPart](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_UploadPart.html) API operation sends one part and returns an entity tag, **ETag**. Its **Content-Length** header covers that part only.\n3. **CompleteMultipartUpload** sends the ordered part numbers and **ETag** values. Amazon S3 joins the parts and calculates the object's size.\n\nEach part must meet the following requirements:\n\n- Every part except the last must be between 5 MiB and 5 GiB. The last part can be smaller than 5 MiB.\n- An upload can have at most 10,000 parts.\n\nIn the example, a Unix pipe connects two programs. A producer writes data to standard output (**stdout**). A writer reads it from standard input (**stdin**), buffers it, and uploads each full buffer as a part","url":"https://repost.aws/articles/ARtxZoGCNJTPCaRofhH2UfTQ/streaming-data-of-unknown-size-to-amazon-s3-with-multipart-upload"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"How do I find and delete incomplete multipart uploads in Amazon S3?","context":"Short description\n-----------------\n\nIf the [CompleteMultipartUpload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_CompleteMultipartUpload.html) API action request doesn't successfully complete, then Amazon S3 doesn't assemble previously uploaded parts or create a new object. The parts remain in your Amazon S3 storage until the multipart upload completes or cancels. Amazon S3 stores the parts from the incomplete multipart uploads and charges your AWS account for the storage costs.\n\nResolution\n----------\n\n**Note:** If you receive errors when you run AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) commands, then see [Troubleshooting errors for the AWS CLI](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-chap-troubleshooting.html). Also, make sure that [you're using the most recent AWS CLI version](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html)","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/s3-incomplete-multipart-uploads"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Filtering data events by using advanced event selectors","context":"### Example 3: Filtering on the `resources.type` and `eventName` fields to exclude individual objects deleted by an Amazon S3 DeleteObjects event\n\nThe following example shows how to include all data events for all Amazon S3\nobjects in a specific general purpose Amazon S3 bucket but exclude the individual\nobjects deleted by the `DeleteObject` operation. The value for S3\nevents for the `resources.type` field is\n`AWS::S3::Object`. The value for the event name is\n`DeleteObject`.\n\n```\naws cloudtrail put-event-selectors \\\n--trail-name trailName \\\n--advanced-event-selectors \\\n\n{\n \"Name\": \"Exclude Events for DeleteObject operation\",\n \"FieldSelectors\": [\n {\n \"Field\": \"eventCategory\",\n \"Equals\": [\n \"Data\"\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Field\": \"resources.type\",\n \"Equals\": [\n \"AWS::S3::Object\"\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Field\": \"eventName\",\n \"NotEquals\": [\n \"DeleteObject\"\n ]\n }\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Name\": \"Exclude DeleteObject Events for individual objects deleted by DeleteObjects Operation\",\n \"FieldSelectors\": [\n {\n \"Field\": \"eventCategory\",\n \"Equals\": [\n \"Data\"\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Field\": \"resources.type\",\n \"Equals\": [\n \"AWS::S3::Object\"\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Field\": \"eventName\",\n \"Equals\": [\n \"DeleteObject\"\n ]\n },\n {\n \"Field\": \"eventType\",\n \"NotEquals\": [\n \"AwsServiceEvent\"\n ]\n }\n ]\n }\n] (edited)\n\n```","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/filtering-data-events.html"}]}}

