{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions","skill_description":"Deploy an event-driven workflow that routes S3 uploads to either Lambda or Fargate via Step Functions based on file size. Uses EventBridge to trigger a Step Functions state machine when objects are uploaded to S3. Small files are processed by Lambda, large files by a Fargate task. Includes VPC, ECR repository, ECS cluster, and scoped IAM roles. Trigger keywords: Step Functions, Fargate, Lambda, S3 event, EventBridge, ECS, ECR, file processing, workflow orchestration, serverless.\n\n\nServices: step-functions, lambda, s3, eventbridge, ecs, ecr, fargate\nTasks: deploy, configure\nPersona: backend-developer\nWorkload: serverless, containers\n*","skill_name":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking whether multipart S3 uploads use different CloudTrail event names than PutObject."}
